WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.904 --> 00:00:18.000
On Journey to the Microcosmos, we love microbes.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:23.846
We love to see them swim and wander and live
out their unique lives.

00:00:23.846 --> 00:00:31.735
But we’ve also had to make peace with the
fact that the microcosmos is not a peaceful utopia.

00:00:31.735 --> 00:00:39.170
This is a world where microbes are both residents
and food, which means that occasionally, we’ll

00:00:39.170 --> 00:00:45.590
have to spend our time together watching organisms,
whose bodies are fractions upon fractions

00:00:45.590 --> 00:00:51.965
upon fractions of a millimeter in size, turn
into vicious predators.

00:00:51.965 --> 00:00:57.840
So today, in honor of both predator and prey,
we’re going to be revisiting some of our

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:02.200
favorite videos about eating in the microcosmos.

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:06.000
Grab a snack, and join our microbes as they
snack.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.479
And we’ll start by revisiting some of the
clever

00:01:08.479 --> 00:01:12.000
strategies that microbes use to gather their
prey.

00:01:17.427 --> 00:01:24.330
Despite huge differences in morphology and
biological structures, all living organisms

00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:27.150
do the same three basic things;

00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:31.704
they get food, digest it, and excrete waste
materials.

00:01:31.704 --> 00:01:35.514
Living organisms require energy to live.

00:01:35.514 --> 00:01:40.640
Some produce their own food, usually through
photosynthesis, we call these autotrophs,

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:43.810
but many organisms cannot make their own food.

00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:47.885
We call these heterotrophs and they...eat.

00:01:50.770 --> 00:01:57.000
Among heterotrophic single-celled eukaryotes,
food is taken into the cell in various methods

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:01.149
but once it’s there, it's wrapped by
a membrane

00:02:01.149 --> 00:02:04.583
and forms something called a food vacuole.

00:02:04.583 --> 00:02:10.973
Then the cell flushes digestive enzymes inside
the food vacuole to start the digestion.

00:02:10.973 --> 00:02:16.190
Nutrients are taken into the cytoplasm and
the waste material left in the vacuole then

00:02:16.190 --> 00:02:20.810
basically fuses with the outer
membrane of the cell and what's left in the

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:24.513
vacuole is discharged into the environment

00:02:24.513 --> 00:02:30.340
In some cases, like inside this beautiful
single-celled Nassula ornata which feeds on

00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:36.160
filamentous cyanobacteria, content of the
food vacuole reacts with the digestive enzymes

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:37.605
and changes color.

00:02:37.605 --> 00:02:39.716
But that process takes time.

00:02:39.716 --> 00:02:45.100
Because each vacuole formed at a different
time, they are in different stages of digestion

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:49.040
which gives this cell it’s colorful polka
dots.

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:55.195
But how do these heterotrophic organisms get
their food?

00:02:55.195 --> 00:02:59.500
Well, in spite of a remarkable
amount of diversity, a lot of microorganisms

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:03.510
use one of the same three strategies for getting
their food.

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:06.590
Some of this will be familiar to the macro
world

00:03:06.590 --> 00:03:08.612
some of it will not.

00:03:10.330 --> 00:03:13.080
One of the less familiar is filter feeding

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:21.060
which allows larger organisms to consume suspended
food particles or much smaller organisms.

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:25.629
There are filter-feeders in the macro-world,
baleen whales come to mind.

00:03:25.629 --> 00:03:32.330
But while a whale must swim through giant
clouds of small organisms, in the microcosmos,

00:03:32.330 --> 00:03:34.692
your food can come to you.

00:03:36.212 --> 00:03:42.580
Some filter feeders use hair-like structures
called cilia to create a vortex that brings

00:03:42.580 --> 00:03:47.430
other microorganisms or food particles to
the cell mouth.

00:03:47.430 --> 00:03:53.280
These cilia are specialized for this task
and their beating creates a current that expediently

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:59.370
and beautifully directs every nearby thing
into the waiting mouth of the microorganism.

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:04.963
The cilia are often too small for us to see,
but you can see their effects.

00:04:04.963 --> 00:04:06.846
Take a look at these Paramecia.

00:04:06.846 --> 00:04:09.620
They're consuming tiny, tiny bacteria.

00:04:09.620 --> 00:04:15.269
and you can see their cilia causing small
organisms to tumble across them.

00:04:15.269 --> 00:04:18.370
You can also see all of their food vacuoles
on the inside,

00:04:18.370 --> 00:04:22.860
and if you look very carefully, you can see
a new food vacuole forming,

00:04:22.860 --> 00:04:26.359
getting filled up with those tiny, bacterial
cells.

00:04:30.764 --> 00:04:36.850
Some of the best and most obvious filter feeders
are rotifers, micro-animals that use cilia

00:04:36.850 --> 00:04:40.576
to create swirling vortices around their mouth
parts.

00:04:40.576 --> 00:04:46.276
You can see how successful this feeding strategy
is by it’s belly full of algae cells.

00:04:46.276 --> 00:04:51.233
Every time its mouth fills with more algea,
it contracts to swallow the food.

00:04:54.250 --> 00:04:58.681
Now observe these single-celled organisms
called stentors,

00:04:58.681 --> 00:05:02.045
they are much bigger than most other microorganisms.

00:05:02.045 --> 00:05:04.190
You can actually see them with the naked eye.

00:05:04.190 --> 00:05:10.143
and they also use filter feeding to push all
of their algal food into their cell mouths.

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:21.350
Our second feeding mechanism, maybe the most
familiar,

00:05:21.350 --> 00:05:25.280
and the most exciting is called raptorial
feeding.

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:31.693
Raptorial feeders selectively capture prey
and hunt other organisms.

00:05:31.693 --> 00:05:34.660
In this video you can see Dileptus hunting.

00:05:34.660 --> 00:05:39.370
It paralyzes one organism with the touch of
its trunk-like proboscis, and then it pulls

00:05:39.370 --> 00:05:41.470
that organism into itself

00:05:41.470 --> 00:05:44.569
in a process called phagocytosis.

00:05:48.974 --> 00:05:53.810
Many of these microorganisms are armed with
something called toxicysts.

00:05:53.810 --> 00:05:58.900
These are little harpoon-like structures filled
with toxins and they are located on a particular

00:05:58.900 --> 00:06:03.120
part of the cell which the microorganism uses
for hunting.

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:08.550
These tiny harpoons are then fired when they
come in contact with prey organisms

00:06:08.550 --> 00:06:10.846
which then become immobilized.

00:06:12.278 --> 00:06:17.310
This is Bursaria, it’s a single-celled organism
with a huge mouth, and things have not gone

00:06:17.310 --> 00:06:21.145
well for the Paramecium that is now inside
it.

00:06:21.145 --> 00:06:27.630
The Paramecium dies immediately because of
the toxicysts on the inside of the Bursaria,

00:06:27.630 --> 00:06:29.415
so at least it was quick.

00:06:32.432 --> 00:06:37.946
Now get ready for some truly gorgeous footage
of a micro-animal’s day going south.

00:06:37.946 --> 00:06:44.400
First Paradileptus immobilizes the rotifer
with fired toxicysts and the animal is swallowed

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:47.705
by the single-celled organism
as it swims away.

00:06:52.374 --> 00:06:58.180
This is a Frontonia which is a close relative
of Paramecia, but lacks the filter-feeding

00:06:58.180 --> 00:07:05.370
habits of its relatives and feeds predaciously
on large diatoms and filamentous cyanobacteria

00:07:05.370 --> 00:07:07.736
such as Oscillatoria here.

00:07:07.736 --> 00:07:13.440
Though, in this case, it turns out this Frontonia
bit off a little more than he could chew.

00:07:14.696 --> 00:07:18.470
Another raptorial feeding style is called
histophagy.

00:07:18.470 --> 00:07:25.660
Histophagous organisms such as these single-celled
Coleps attack injured but live animals or

00:07:25.660 --> 00:07:29.599
other single-celled organisms,
sucking off hunks of tissue

00:07:29.599 --> 00:07:32.399
rather than consuming whole organisms.

00:07:32.399 --> 00:07:38.449
When they attack an animal, they enter wounds
and ingest tissue often attacking in groups

00:07:38.449 --> 00:07:43.729
because their chemical sensing abilities attract
many of them from a distance,

00:07:43.729 --> 00:07:46.005
like microscopic vultures.

00:07:46.005 --> 00:07:53.169
When a number of them gather in one place,
it’s hard to avoid another macro-world analog...piranhas,

00:07:53.169 --> 00:07:57.210
devouring everything soft in no time at all.

00:08:00.800 --> 00:08:05.500
There is a huge variety of raptorial feeding,
this is just the beginning, but we wanted

00:08:05.500 --> 00:08:08.690
to show you one more before we move on.

00:08:08.690 --> 00:08:13.370
This is Vampyrella, an amoeba with a suitable
name.

00:08:13.370 --> 00:08:16.490
It specializes in feeding on filamentous
algae.

00:08:16.490 --> 00:08:20.046
First, it bores a hole through the algal cell
wall and

00:08:20.046 --> 00:08:25.823
then slurps out the gooey, nutritious cytoplasm.

00:08:33.928 --> 00:08:36.909
Our final feeding mechanism, for today at
least,

00:08:36.909 --> 00:08:38.839
is diffusion feeding,

00:08:38.839 --> 00:08:42.240
in which the predator just sits in the same
place, relying

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:45.912
on the prey to accidentally make contact.

00:08:45.912 --> 00:08:51.930
This is a Heliozoa, it’s a single celled
amoeboid and because of its resemblance to

00:08:51.930 --> 00:08:59.130
the sun due to the rays coming out of its
cell, it’s sometimes called the “sun animalcule”.

00:08:59.130 --> 00:09:01.780
The rays are called axopodia.

00:09:01.780 --> 00:09:06.970
These are sometimes used in locomotion and,
in this case, for hunting prey.

00:09:07.872 --> 00:09:13.957
Axopodia, are cytoplasmic extensions, meaning
they’re a part of the cell membrane, even

00:09:13.957 --> 00:09:16.506
though they look like they’re sticking out
of it.

00:09:16.506 --> 00:09:18.500
Each one has a central supporting

00:09:18.500 --> 00:09:23.130
rod of microtubules that gives it this rigid
structure.

00:09:23.130 --> 00:09:29.630
The axopodia are coated in organelles that
discharge toxins when touched, which impair

00:09:29.630 --> 00:09:33.277
or even paralyze Heliozoa's prey.

00:09:33.277 --> 00:09:39.959
After the organism is captured, those microtubules
are drawn back into the cell, thus retracting

00:09:39.959 --> 00:09:47.480
the axopodia and allowing the cell to swallow
the unlucky organism, or prey is just engulfed

00:09:47.480 --> 00:09:51.720
by extrusions from the cell called pseudopodia.

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:58.339
In this video, a rotifer has been captured
by a Heliozoa and it is slowly getting eaten by it.

00:09:58.339 --> 00:10:00.593
Now this is something that happens fairly
freuently,

00:10:00.593 --> 00:10:03.779
but we did capture something unusal here.

00:10:03.779 --> 00:10:11.516
While it was stuck to Heliozoa's axopodia,
this rotifer actually lays its egg.

00:10:11.516 --> 00:10:16.235
But neither egg nor the rotifer is going to
escape this.

00:10:16.235 --> 00:10:18.000
Surprisingly heartbreaking.

00:10:19.426 --> 00:10:25.060
This is a Suctorian, it is a ciliate just
like Paramecium and Stentor.

00:10:25.060 --> 00:10:30.720
These organisms have hair-like cilia during
the early stage of their life, but as adults

00:10:30.720 --> 00:10:33.859
they develop bundles of tentacles.

00:10:33.859 --> 00:10:40.670
Just like in Heliozoa these tentacles are
supported by an internal cylinder of microtubules.

00:10:40.670 --> 00:10:46.930
The tip of the tentacles have extrusomes;
these are special structures that attach to

00:10:46.930 --> 00:10:51.014
and immobilize any other ciliates that touch
them.

00:10:51.014 --> 00:10:56.300
The tentacles eventually penetrate the cell
membrane of the prey, and then the contents

00:10:56.300 --> 00:11:01.310
of the prey is sucked out through the tentacle.

00:11:01.310 --> 00:11:08.180
In the clip, a suctorian has caught 4 individual
Vorticella with its tentacles and is slowly

00:11:08.180 --> 00:11:10.339
sucking their cytoplasm.

00:11:10.339 --> 00:11:15.231
It looks a little like the vorticella have
the suctorian surrounded, but in fact, they

00:11:15.231 --> 00:11:17.888
are powerless to escape it.

00:11:17.888 --> 00:11:19.959
It’s a dangerous world out there.

00:11:19.959 --> 00:11:26.240
The complex chemicals created by organisms
to sustain their life necessarily are useful

00:11:26.240 --> 00:11:31.440
to other organisms, as building blocks and
as fuel.

00:11:31.440 --> 00:11:33.770
And so predation evolved.

00:11:33.770 --> 00:11:37.871
It’s beautiful, it’s constant, and it’s
brutal.

00:11:45.360 --> 00:11:50.889
Now that we’ve seen how the hunters of the
microcosmos get their food, let’s experience

00:11:50.889 --> 00:11:55.962
the more challenging part of the predator-prey
dynamic: getting eaten.

00:11:55.962 --> 00:12:01.889
For the organisms in our next video, finding
themselves in the belly of the beast is just

00:12:01.889 --> 00:12:08.928
the start of a more grueling process, one
that converts their bodies into nutrients.

00:12:15.910 --> 00:12:19.660
Tardigrades are famous for their capacity
to survive.

00:12:19.660 --> 00:12:24.970
If you look them up, you will be inundated
with long lists of the many things scientists

00:12:24.970 --> 00:12:28.920
have thrown their way to see if the tardigrade
will survive.

00:12:28.920 --> 00:12:32.209
We’ve made some lists like those ourselves.

00:12:32.209 --> 00:12:34.826
Tardigrades on the moon, tardigrades in extreme
heat.

00:12:34.826 --> 00:12:35.660
You’ve heard about them.

00:12:35.660 --> 00:12:37.076
We’ve talked about them.

00:12:37.076 --> 00:12:38.568
What else is there?

00:12:38.568 --> 00:12:41.550
Well, here’s a surprising fact you might
not have known.

00:12:41.550 --> 00:12:46.190
For certain species of tardigrades, like the
one in the middle of your screen right now,

00:12:46.190 --> 00:12:52.066
you can tell the difference between a male
and a female by looking at their toes.

00:12:52.066 --> 00:12:53.082
That’s right.

00:12:53.082 --> 00:12:54.532
The toes.

00:12:54.532 --> 00:12:59.339
These tardigrades are called Milnesium, and
the males of these species have a pair of

00:12:59.339 --> 00:13:03.353
claws on their feet that are shaped a little
bit differently from the rest of the claws,

00:13:03.353 --> 00:13:07.266
with only one hook on each claw instead of
the usual two.

00:13:07.266 --> 00:13:14.412
But after taking a closer peek at this tardigrade’s
toes, we can confirm that she is a female tardigrade.

00:13:14.412 --> 00:13:16.910
Now, that’s a fun fact for us as we watch
the clip.

00:13:16.910 --> 00:13:21.790
But it does not actually do anything for the
poor rotifers that are surrounding this tardigrade,

00:13:21.790 --> 00:13:27.448
who are currently stretching themselves in
and out of danger.

00:13:27.448 --> 00:13:33.320
Around the tip of a tardigrade’s mouth are
small bumps that we think act like little sensors.

00:13:33.320 --> 00:13:39.350
And when those bumps make contact with a rotifer,
out pops the tardigrade’s stylet—a needle

00:13:39.350 --> 00:13:42.542
that pokes out from the tubular mouth opening.

00:13:42.542 --> 00:13:48.110
That stylet pokes into the rotifer, and from
there the tardigrade uses a circular structure

00:13:48.110 --> 00:13:52.709
called a pharynx in its head to create strong
suction.

00:13:52.709 --> 00:13:57.709
And then, the stylet goes from functioning
as a needle to functioning as a portable,

00:13:57.709 --> 00:14:03.950
built-in straw that lets the tardigrade suck
out the inside of its prey.

00:14:03.950 --> 00:14:08.670
You can see some of the bodily fluid leaving
the rotifer as the tardigrade’s stylet pumps

00:14:08.670 --> 00:14:10.709
away like a beating heart.

00:14:10.709 --> 00:14:16.286
And when she is done, the rotifer remains,
like an empy coffee cup.

00:14:20.229 --> 00:14:24.610
This method of eating works quite well for
the tardigrade, letting her eat quickly from

00:14:24.610 --> 00:14:26.680
the prey that is directly in front of her.

00:14:26.680 --> 00:14:31.977
But not all organisms come equipped with a
weapon that lets them turn rotifers into Capri Suns.

00:14:31.977 --> 00:14:37.290
These organisms have to turn to other methods
to extract nutrients from their food.

00:14:37.290 --> 00:14:42.550
This marine ciliate is called kentrophyllum,
and it came to James, our master of microscopes,

00:14:42.550 --> 00:14:45.264
in a large container full of beach sand.

00:14:45.264 --> 00:14:49.839
On a normal day, maybe we would talk more
about its funny almond shape that stretches

00:14:49.839 --> 00:14:51.322
as it swims around.

00:14:51.322 --> 00:14:55.930
But today is not a normal day because this
kentrophyllum is about to be overshadowed

00:14:55.930 --> 00:14:57.776
by a rotifer.

00:14:57.776 --> 00:15:00.216
Now, where is the rotifer, you might ask?

00:15:00.216 --> 00:15:03.979
It is inside the kentrophyllum, of course.

00:15:03.979 --> 00:15:08.620
We did not catch the moment of its capture,
when the toxic needles lining the kentrophyllum

00:15:08.620 --> 00:15:10.920
darted out and paralyzed the rotifer.

00:15:10.920 --> 00:15:17.358
By the time we arrived, the rotifer had already
been ingested…but it was not dead yet.

00:15:20.970 --> 00:15:26.540
You can see it wiggling and wrestling at the
broad end of the kentrophyllum, causing the

00:15:26.540 --> 00:15:29.790
ciliate’s body to wrinkle and fold in on
itself.

00:15:29.790 --> 00:15:35.350
But it is trapped inside of a food vacuole
now, a compartment that exists to break this

00:15:35.350 --> 00:15:36.680
rotifer down.

00:15:36.680 --> 00:15:41.230
Sure, the rotifer can try and fight against
the walls of the vacuole holding it hostage.

00:15:41.230 --> 00:15:47.149
But there’s not much it can do against the
digestive enzymes pouring in, or the increasing

00:15:47.149 --> 00:15:49.360
acidity of its surroundings.

00:15:49.360 --> 00:15:55.470
A food vacuole is a hostile place to be, and
for minutes, its destructive tools will go

00:15:55.470 --> 00:15:57.243
to work.

00:15:57.243 --> 00:16:03.389
In the end, the rotifer will be left in dissolved
pieces to be absorbed by the kentrophyllum’s

00:16:03.389 --> 00:16:08.743
cytoplasm, sustaining the organism that was
the site of its last battle.

00:16:08.743 --> 00:16:13.303
It’s a lonely death for the rotifer, with
only the kentrophyllum to witness it.

00:16:13.303 --> 00:16:22.440
Though I suppose now there is also us, sharing
and immortalizing that rotifer’s last moments.

00:16:22.440 --> 00:16:27.259
And if the rotifer had not died in the body
of another organism, it may have ended up

00:16:27.259 --> 00:16:29.003
like this gastrotrich.

00:16:29.003 --> 00:16:34.569
Now to be fair, they did find themselves in
a very similar situation: they are both dead.

00:16:34.569 --> 00:16:37.680
But the rotifer was eaten alive, 
digested from the outside 

00:16:37.680 --> 00:16:39.825
 while trapped in another organism.

00:16:39.825 --> 00:16:44.920
Meanwhile, this gastrotrich is going through
the exact opposite situation.

00:16:44.920 --> 00:16:50.205
It is being eaten from the inside out, by
a scavenger called a peranema.

00:16:50.205 --> 00:16:55.040
Now, as far as we know, the peranema didn’t
do anything to kill the gastrotrich.

00:16:55.040 --> 00:16:58.997
It was likely already dead, though we don’t
know what killed it.

00:16:58.997 --> 00:17:02.014
But the peranema is both a hunter and a scavenger.

00:17:02.014 --> 00:17:08.390
Sometimes it can be found with other peranema
as they hunt down prey, in a pack of hungry microbes.

00:17:08.390 --> 00:17:11.160
At other times, the peranema scavenges.

00:17:11.160 --> 00:17:15.689
They are notorious for being able to squeeze
their way into whatever holes they find to

00:17:15.689 --> 00:17:17.740
get into a dead organism.

00:17:17.740 --> 00:17:23.360
And in this case, the gastrotrich’s exoskeleton
was the right combination of available, open,

00:17:23.360 --> 00:17:29.050
and dead for the peranema, which found its
way in and decided to begin helping itself

00:17:29.050 --> 00:17:32.010
to the buffet of dead tissue around it.

00:17:32.010 --> 00:17:36.919
After all, the gastrotrich won’t be needing
that tissue anymore, so the peranema might

00:17:36.919 --> 00:17:39.470
as well make good use of it.

00:17:39.470 --> 00:17:43.016
The gastrotrich’s body has likely vanished
by now.

00:17:43.016 --> 00:17:47.500
Perhaps the peranema will have finished it
off entirely, or some other scavenger will

00:17:47.500 --> 00:17:49.550
have joined in on the fun.

00:17:49.550 --> 00:17:54.567
Or perhaps, it will have simply faded, like
the rotifer inside the kentrophyllum, its

00:17:54.567 --> 00:17:59.644
body eventually fading into the world that
encapsulates it.

00:17:59.644 --> 00:18:05.100
But for this last moment, let’s remember
the gastrotrich in a more beautiful moment

00:18:05.100 --> 00:18:11.754
under a fluorescent light, glowing in the
glorious purple autofluorescence of its remains.

00:18:19.110 --> 00:18:25.169
If you think that microbes are the only organisms
that eat microbes, think again.

00:18:25.169 --> 00:18:31.993
We humans have a particular fondness for one
specific fungus called Saccharomyces cerevisiae,

00:18:31.993 --> 00:18:35.334
but you probably know better as “yeast.”

00:18:35.334 --> 00:18:40.633
Often when we talk about the invisible role
that microbes have played in human history,

00:18:40.633 --> 00:18:44.146
we’re talking about the destruction wrought
by diseases.

00:18:44.146 --> 00:18:50.110
But as we’ll talk about in the next video,
the life of Saccharomyces cerevisiae has intertwined

00:18:50.110 --> 00:18:55.142
with our own in ways that are both delicious
and mysterious.

00:19:03.468 --> 00:19:09.630
Every time you eat a piece of bread or drink
a glass of beer, you are participating in

00:19:09.630 --> 00:19:16.039
what might be (depending on how strict you
are about your definitions) one of the longest-running

00:19:16.039 --> 00:19:20.980
microbiology experiments in human history.

00:19:20.980 --> 00:19:26.320
While the earliest scientific studies of microbes
usually go back only a few centuries

00:19:26.320 --> 00:19:28.590
when it comes to yeast, the microbe

00:19:28.590 --> 00:19:34.120
at the core of some of our favorite foods
and drinks, well...those past few centuries

00:19:34.120 --> 00:19:36.740
barely scratch the surface.

00:19:36.740 --> 00:19:42.350
But before we get to that history, let’s
start with some of the basic biology.

00:19:42.350 --> 00:19:47.730
Yeast are a fungus, though unlike many in
that kingdom, they don’t grow the branching

00:19:47.730 --> 00:19:51.179
hyphae that characterize organisms like mushrooms.

00:19:51.179 --> 00:19:58.390
Instead, yeast grow primarily through a process
of asexual reproduction called “budding.”.

00:19:58.390 --> 00:20:05.159
While there are many yeast species found among
different fungal phyla, perhaps the most well-known

00:20:05.159 --> 00:20:12.279
is Saccharomyces cerevisiae, which you might
see labelled as brewer’s yeast or baker’s yeast.

00:20:12.279 --> 00:20:17.710
Along with a few other yeasts, these various
strains of S. cerevisiae are what we’ve

00:20:17.710 --> 00:20:25.323
come to rely on for our beer, wine, and bread--and
all through the magic of fermentation.

00:20:25.323 --> 00:20:31.900
Yeast, like many organisms, rely on different
metabolic pathways to break down sugar and

00:20:31.900 --> 00:20:33.679
create energy.

00:20:33.679 --> 00:20:37.130
These processes usually rely on oxygen.

00:20:37.130 --> 00:20:42.340
But to deal with situations where oxygen may
be less readily available or some of their

00:20:42.340 --> 00:20:48.993
key metabolic processes are shut down, yeast
turn to alcoholic fermentation.

00:20:48.993 --> 00:20:55.010
Fermentation helps the yeast get the energy
it needs, but the particular chemical path

00:20:55.010 --> 00:21:00.309
they follow also produces ethanol and carbon
dioxide.

00:21:00.309 --> 00:21:06.750
The yeast doesn’t need these byproducts—in
fact, too much ethanol could be toxic for them.

00:21:06.750 --> 00:21:13.940
But what is waste to a yeast is treasure to
us, and that shift in metabolic activity has

00:21:13.940 --> 00:21:20.332
been making our bread rise and our brains
woozy for millennia.

00:21:22.270 --> 00:21:28.470
Molecular studies of pottery jars found in
China found that they may have held some kind

00:21:28.470 --> 00:21:34.593
of fermented beverage as far back as 7000
BCE.

00:21:34.593 --> 00:21:40.980
And archaeological evidence has turned up
for leavened bread from 2000 BCE in Egypt

00:21:40.980 --> 00:21:44.246
and 1000 BCE in North Western China.

00:21:44.246 --> 00:21:48.580
It’s easy to see why our use of yeast has
persisted for millennia.

00:21:48.580 --> 00:21:52.833
To quote one group of scientists, it helped
us make food and drinks with a

00:21:52.833 --> 00:21:58.077
“enriched sensorial palate” and also 
a “euphoriant effect.”

00:21:59.421 --> 00:22:06.773
But the long-standing ubiquity of yeast also
emphasizes how mysterious their origins are.

00:22:06.773 --> 00:22:10.840
We don’t actually know how yeast became
involved with our cooking.

00:22:10.840 --> 00:22:17.210
We just know that it was ancient, likely starting
with wine, and would come to span continents,

00:22:17.210 --> 00:22:20.130
potentially spreading through trade.

00:22:20.130 --> 00:22:24.990
The results were food and drinks produced
through a series of observations about how

00:22:24.990 --> 00:22:30.620
factors like temperature, time, and air might
affect the final product.

00:22:30.620 --> 00:22:37.650
To quote Samuel Johnson’s 18th century dictionary,
barm--a type of yeast--is “the ferment put

00:22:37.650 --> 00:22:43.072
into drink to make it work, and into bread
to lighten and swell it.”

00:22:43.072 --> 00:22:49.540
At the time they didn’t know what was actually
in yeast or how it worked to shape those results.

00:22:49.540 --> 00:22:55.321
We just knew it was a thing that provided…something…somehow….

00:22:55.321 --> 00:23:00.940
Moreover, the earliest glimpses of yeast under
the microscope seemed to support the idea

00:23:00.940 --> 00:23:07.090
that yeast was just some sort of chemical,
a fermenting agent, not a biological entity

00:23:07.090 --> 00:23:08.480
of its own.

00:23:08.480 --> 00:23:12.870
When Antoni van Leeuwenhoek observed yeast
in the 17th century, he thought they were

00:23:12.870 --> 00:23:16.386
just bits of globular particles.

00:23:16.386 --> 00:23:20.929
Against the new and incredible bits of life
he was seeing for the first time through the

00:23:20.929 --> 00:23:26.680
microscope, yeast seemed useful but ultimately
unliving.

00:23:26.680 --> 00:23:32.300
But over the next few centuries, as more scientists
worked to try and understand how fermentation

00:23:32.300 --> 00:23:39.210
actually worked, they identified not just
the chemical reactions that shaped the process,

00:23:39.210 --> 00:23:44.700
but that yeast was an actual living organism
essential to fermentation.

00:23:44.700 --> 00:23:50.188
Eventually, Louis Pasteur would put all the
pieces together, publishing a paper titled

00:23:50.188 --> 00:23:56.029
“The Memoir of the Fermentation of Alcohol”,
or something like that, but in French.

00:23:56.029 --> 00:24:03.470
It was published in 1857 and it established
the interplay between chemistry and biology

00:24:03.470 --> 00:24:09.077
that allowed yeast to turn to alcoholic fermentation
to survive.

00:24:09.077 --> 00:24:14.419
He would also later uncover another form of
fermentation carried out by bacteria, which

00:24:14.419 --> 00:24:17.429
is called lactic acid fermentation.

00:24:17.429 --> 00:24:23.390
Pasteur’s work didn’t just change our
understanding of how bread was made, it would

00:24:23.390 --> 00:24:30.190
go on to inform our understanding of germ
theory, and how the invisible world of microbes

00:24:30.190 --> 00:24:34.630
connects to the grim realities of disease.

00:24:34.630 --> 00:24:40.090
From this early history, yeast have gone on
to become perhaps one of the most studied

00:24:40.090 --> 00:24:44.590
eukaryotic organisms in modern biological
research.

00:24:44.590 --> 00:24:50.430
Saccharomyces cerevisiae in particular has
had an illustrious research career.

00:24:50.430 --> 00:24:57.280
In 1996, its genome was fully sequenced, making
it the first eukaryotic organism to be genetically

00:24:57.280 --> 00:24:58.888
mapped out.

00:24:58.888 --> 00:25:04.460
Part of what makes yeast such great research
organisms is that they are easy to grow and

00:25:04.460 --> 00:25:10.340
manipulate--both through environmental and
genetic means--which has allowed us to use

00:25:10.340 --> 00:25:15.847
them to study everything from DNA replication
to prion diseases.

00:25:15.847 --> 00:25:21.619
In the same way that cultures have refined
the ways we use yeast in our food and drinks,

00:25:21.619 --> 00:25:26.857
scientists have refined the way we use yeast
in our lab experiments.

00:25:26.857 --> 00:25:31.670
They’ve even developed genetic engineering
techniques that repurpose yeast to brew the

00:25:31.670 --> 00:25:37.260
ingredients for everything from perfumes to
antimalarial drugs.

00:25:37.260 --> 00:25:42.559
When you consider just how far we’ve come
with yeast, you might begin to wonder: did

00:25:42.559 --> 00:25:45.770
we domesticate them?

00:25:45.770 --> 00:25:52.080
After all, our early days of yeast use likely
coincided with our early days of plant and

00:25:52.080 --> 00:25:54.140
animal domestication.

00:25:54.140 --> 00:25:59.730
So in the same way that we’ve come to rely
on domesticated animals for food and labor,

00:25:59.730 --> 00:26:04.336
maybe yeast are our microbial workhorses.

00:26:04.336 --> 00:26:10.200
The answer to that question is one scientists
are still studying, in part because the history

00:26:10.200 --> 00:26:18.309
of yeast is so large and mysterious, and in
part because domestication has a very specific

00:26:18.309 --> 00:26:20.870
definition in these conversations.

00:26:20.870 --> 00:26:27.919
One paper we found describes it as “human
selection and breeding of wild species to

00:26:27.919 --> 00:26:35.080
obtain cultivated variants that thrive in
man-made environments, but behave sub-optimally

00:26:35.080 --> 00:26:40.230
in nature,” now that’s quite a number
of nuanced requirements to check off.

00:26:40.230 --> 00:26:45.960
But with this definition in mind, beer yeast
seems to show the most evidence for having

00:26:45.960 --> 00:26:51.830
been domesticated, which is likely connected
to specific aspects of the brewing process

00:26:51.830 --> 00:26:56.960
that have limited the exposure of the domesticated
yeast to wild yeast.

00:26:56.960 --> 00:27:04.010
But all yeasts considered, the genetic analysis
of beer yeast strains show that this domestication

00:27:04.010 --> 00:27:09.950
is actually relatively recent—as in, it
only happened a few centuries ago.

00:27:09.950 --> 00:27:16.070
While it did happen before we even knew that
yeast was a living thing that we were domesticating,

00:27:16.070 --> 00:27:22.010
it still came long after we started using
yeast to make beer, leaving much of the organism’s

00:27:22.010 --> 00:27:25.750
history still a mystery.

00:27:25.750 --> 00:27:32.049
It is often tempting to think that we have
a microbial world and a human world that intersect

00:27:32.049 --> 00:27:35.940
only in chemical reactions and biological
connections.

00:27:35.940 --> 00:27:43.110
But yeast reminds us that microbes are more
than just a scientific reality, they are a

00:27:43.110 --> 00:27:47.630
cultural one--even when we didn’t know they
were around.

00:27:47.630 --> 00:27:55.500
Yeast have created our foods, shaped our traditions,
and both bonded and divided us.

00:27:55.500 --> 00:28:01.640
And how we use and change yeast will only
continue to shape our lives.

00:28:01.640 --> 00:28:08.290
Their future, tied so much to our own, is
every bit as grand and mysterious as their

00:28:08.290 --> 00:28:09.776
past has been.

00:28:14.952 --> 00:28:20.460
There is but one inevitable way to end all
this, with poop.

00:28:20.460 --> 00:28:23.221
Because yes, even microbes do it.

00:28:23.221 --> 00:28:28.640
Not all that we eat can be retained in our
bodies, something must exit.

00:28:28.640 --> 00:28:34.519
So come for the funny videos of microbes pooping,
stay for the extra lesson on how they do it

00:28:34.519 --> 00:28:35.841
without a butthole.

00:28:41.413 --> 00:28:45.754
Ah, food, one of life’s great pleasures.

00:28:45.754 --> 00:28:50.970
Whether you’re an organism whose body can
create its own food, or you make do with the

00:28:50.970 --> 00:28:58.043
consumption of other organisms, food sustains
and connects life both large and small.

00:28:58.043 --> 00:29:00.281
But we’re not going to talk about food today.

00:29:00.281 --> 00:29:03.850
We’re going to talk about what happens…after.

00:29:03.850 --> 00:29:08.244
No great meal comes without consequences.

00:29:08.244 --> 00:29:14.113
Even photosynthetic microbes produce oxygen
they don’t need, a byproduct that is quite

00:29:14.113 --> 00:29:18.350
useful to many other organisms, including
us.

00:29:18.350 --> 00:29:23.450
And for organisms that have to digest their
food to extract nutrients, well, there’s

00:29:23.450 --> 00:29:28.039
all sorts of stuff left behind when the process
is done.

00:29:28.039 --> 00:29:34.460
In the world of the microcosmos, there are
not only many varied tiny organisms.

00:29:34.460 --> 00:29:40.385
There are also many varied ways to produce
even tinier poop.

00:29:46.200 --> 00:29:49.369
And when we say “tinier,” we mean it.

00:29:49.369 --> 00:29:53.770
If you’re worried about being grossed out
by this video, or you really want to make

00:29:53.770 --> 00:29:58.059
sure you’re not missing anything, just know
that some of this pooping will require you

00:29:58.059 --> 00:30:03.169
to pay very close attention.

00:30:03.169 --> 00:30:07.515
Some of this will probably look similar to
macroscopic bowel movements, 

00:30:07.515 --> 00:30:10.030
and some will seem less so.

00:30:10.030 --> 00:30:15.840
So let’s start with the more poop-like poop
that comes from some of the multicellular

00:30:15.840 --> 00:30:22.960
members of the microcosmos, particularly the
ones with digestive systems complete with

00:30:22.960 --> 00:30:28.661
specialized regions for digesting food and
releasing solid waste.

00:30:31.304 --> 00:30:38.360
Our dragon-like friend the gastrotrich is
doing the old scrunch-and-poop, probably after

00:30:38.360 --> 00:30:43.040
having finished a nice meal made up of bacteria
or protozoa.

00:30:43.040 --> 00:30:49.019
The food came in through its pharynx and was
then sent through the intestine, a straight

00:30:49.019 --> 00:30:51.799
tube full of digestive cells.

00:30:51.799 --> 00:30:58.980
After, the remains continued their way to
the end of the tube, where lies the anus--a

00:30:58.980 --> 00:31:04.982
microscopic gateway out of the gastrotrich
and into the world.

00:31:04.982 --> 00:31:10.459
That’s all well and good for the more complex
organisms, but how does one poop when one

00:31:10.459 --> 00:31:13.511
does not have a butthole?

00:31:13.511 --> 00:31:18.983
Well, for some single-celled organisms, one
becomes the butthole.

00:31:23.520 --> 00:31:26.510
Such is the case for amoebas.

00:31:26.510 --> 00:31:34.159
Their formlessness allows them to easily shift
their shape around to take in food via phagocytosis.

00:31:34.159 --> 00:31:40.440
The amoeba here happens to have found a nice
meal made of a ciliate, which is now contained

00:31:40.440 --> 00:31:44.555
in a food vacuole filling with digestive enzymes.

00:31:44.555 --> 00:31:50.940
Those enzymes will eventually break the ciliate
down, allowing the amoeba to absorb nutrients

00:31:50.940 --> 00:31:53.480
into its cytoplasm.

00:31:53.480 --> 00:31:56.438
But then what happens with the remains?

00:31:56.438 --> 00:32:01.310
Well, as we see with this amoeba here, it
gets released.

00:32:01.310 --> 00:32:08.740
Amoebas and other similar organisms use a
process called exocytosis to send waste back

00:32:08.740 --> 00:32:12.237
to the membrane where it is removed from their
body.

00:32:14.307 --> 00:32:20.690
You can see it again here in this Mastigamoeba,
slowly gliding along the microscope slide

00:32:20.690 --> 00:32:24.309
while a round bit seems to come off up top.

00:32:24.309 --> 00:32:30.475
It almost looks like it’s leaving behind
a little piece of itself, which I mean,

00:32:30.475 --> 00:32:31.762
maybe it is.

00:32:33.040 --> 00:32:39.799
Ciliates have a more refined approach to pooping,
which is to say they have the unicellular

00:32:39.799 --> 00:32:44.003
equivalent of an anus called the cytoproct.

00:32:44.003 --> 00:32:51.120
Ciliates gather food into an oral groove,
and then consume it in the digestive vacuoles

00:32:51.120 --> 00:32:52.929
that travel through the cell.

00:32:52.929 --> 00:32:59.809
Eventually, when the vacuole makes its way
to the cytoproct, the contents get out, and

00:32:59.809 --> 00:33:02.006
there you have ciliate poop.

00:33:04.010 --> 00:33:10.280
The way organisms poop—the way microbes
poop—are even more varied and wonderful

00:33:10.280 --> 00:33:12.730
than we’ve been able to show you here.

00:33:12.730 --> 00:33:17.889
But you might be wondering how you even get
from something relatively simple like this

00:33:17.889 --> 00:33:23.630
ciliate releasing a small bit of waste to
something more complex, like this tardigrade

00:33:23.630 --> 00:33:27.621
with its more compartmentalized approach to
defecation.

00:33:27.621 --> 00:33:33.549
Well the answer, of course, is evolution,
but it’s an answer that leads to many more

00:33:33.549 --> 00:33:35.231
unanswered questions.

00:33:35.231 --> 00:33:41.039
Even within animals, the evolution of the
anus—or in some species, the cloaca—is

00:33:41.039 --> 00:33:48.549
not well understood, as discussed in a 2015
review paper with the spectacular title, “Getting

00:33:48.549 --> 00:33:52.264
to the bottom of anal evolution”.

00:33:52.264 --> 00:33:59.110
While the anus itself is linked to the evolution
of a digestive system, not all metazoans have

00:33:59.110 --> 00:34:01.149
a designated anus.

00:34:01.149 --> 00:34:07.400
The hydra gut has only one opening, which
means that the organism’s mouth has to pull

00:34:07.400 --> 00:34:12.119
digestive double duty and open back up to
release the waste.

00:34:12.119 --> 00:34:18.339
And how that system fits in with the evolution
of digestion overall is unclear.

00:34:18.339 --> 00:34:25.700
Much like the evolution of sex, anuses are
a trait that appears and disappears in evolutionary

00:34:25.700 --> 00:34:33.500
lineages, reflecting the fact that they might
be useful for some animals and less so for others.

00:34:33.500 --> 00:34:38.945
The duality of the hydra’s mouth seems to
be working out great for them after all.

00:34:38.945 --> 00:34:45.727
But having a digestive system with a distinct
entrance and exit is pretty handy too.

00:34:45.727 --> 00:34:48.379
For one, it’s just a little less disgusting.

00:34:48.379 --> 00:34:52.300
But it also keeps food flowing in one direction.

00:34:52.300 --> 00:34:58.730
And that means animals like the tardigrade
and us don’t have to wait to finish digesting

00:34:58.730 --> 00:35:00.556
to eat more food.

00:35:00.556 --> 00:35:02.180
That would be annoying.

00:35:02.180 --> 00:35:08.756
We just get to keep eating, all thanks to
that evolutionary mystery that is the anus.

00:35:10.100 --> 00:35:13.800
In the end though, anus or not, everybody
poops.

00:35:13.800 --> 00:35:19.060
Food, after all, might be one of life’s
great pleasures, but all good things must

00:35:19.060 --> 00:35:21.180
come to an end.

00:35:21.180 --> 00:35:27.019
And perhaps, with the microcosmos, we can
find some pleasure in that as well.

00:35:31.864 --> 00:35:38.147
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as 
we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:35:38.147 --> 00:35:43.349
The names on the screen right now, these are
some of our Patreon patrons, the people who

00:35:43.349 --> 00:35:49.890
have decided to directly support this channel
over at patreon.com/journeytomicro, and we

00:35:49.890 --> 00:35:52.994
thank them so much for their continued support.

00:35:54.000 --> 00:35:59.220
If you want to see more from our master of
microscopes, James Weiss, check out Jam &amp; Germs

00:35:59.220 --> 00:36:01.848
on Instagram and TikTok.

00:36:01.848 --> 00:36:07.930
And you can also find Journey to the Microcosmos
on TikTok now if you want some Micro Microcosmos

00:36:07.930 --> 00:36:13.880
a couple times a week, and if you want to
see more from us here, there is always a subscribe

00:36:13.880 --> 00:36:15.904
button somewhere nearby.

